    title: "Famous Five", slug: "famous-five", access_level: "pro",
    short_definition: "Five Canadian women who won the Persons Case in 1929, establishing that women are 'persons' under the law and eligible for the Senate.",
    full_description: "The Famous Five were Emily Murphy, Nellie McClung, Irene Parlby, Louise McKinney, and Henrietta Muir Edwards — all from Alberta. In 1927 they petitioned the Supreme Court of Canada to ask whether women were 'persons' under the British North America Act and therefore qualified to serve in the Senate. The Supreme Court ruled that women were NOT persons. The Famous Five appealed to the British Privy Council, which overturned the ruling on October 18, 1929, declaring that women are indeed persons. This landmark decision was a defining moment in the fight for women's equality in Canada.\n\nThe Famous Five are commemorated with statues on Parliament Hill in Ottawa and on Olympic Plaza in Calgary. Emily Murphy was also the first female magistrate in the British Empire. October 18 is now recognized as Persons Day in Canada.",
    ai_explanation: "Before 1929, the law said women weren't 'persons' — which meant they couldn't be appointed to the Senate or many government positions. The Famous Five were five brave women from Alberta who fought this all the way to the highest court and won. Their victory opened the doors of full legal equality for all Canadian women.",
    related_terms: r([{title:"Charter of Rights and Freedoms",slug:"charter-of-rights-and-freedoms"},{title:"Equality Rights",slug:"equality-rights"},{title:"Senate",slug:"senate"}]),
