    short_definition: "A 1759 battle in Quebec City where British forces defeated the French, leading to British control of New France and shaping Canada's bilingual character.",
    full_description: "The Battle of the Plains of Abraham (September 13, 1759) was a pivotal engagement during the Seven Years' War. British General James Wolfe led his forces up the cliffs of Quebec City in a surprise night attack, defeating the French forces commanded by the Marquis de Montcalm. Both Wolfe and Montcalm died in the battle. The British victory led to the fall of Quebec City and, ultimately, the surrender of New France to Britain through the Treaty of Paris (1763).\n\nThis battle fundamentally shaped Canada's future. Britain now controlled a large French-speaking Catholic population. Rather than attempting to assimilate the Canadiens (French Canadians), Britain passed the Quebec Act of 1774, which protected French civil law, the French language, and the Catholic Church. This accommodation created the foundation for Canada's bilingual, bicultural identity that persists to this day.",
    ai_explanation: "Think of this battle as the moment Canada's bilingual identity was decided. When Britain won in 1759, they had to figure out what to do with 70,000 French-speaking Canadians. Instead of forcing them to become English, Britain let them keep their language, religion, and laws. That tolerance is why Canada is bilingual today.",
