    short_definition: "Canada's highest civilian honour, awarded to Canadians who have made extraordinary contributions to the country.",
    full_description: "The Order of Canada was established in 1967 during Canada's Centennial year. It is the cornerstone of Canada's honours system and recognizes outstanding achievement, dedication to community, and service to Canada. There are three levels: Companion (the highest, for national or international contributions), Officer (for contributions at the national level), and Member (for outstanding service at the local or regional level). Appointments are made by the Governor General on the recommendation of an Advisory Council.\n\nRecipients include scientists, artists, athletes, public servants, and community leaders. Well-known recipients have included Terry Fox (Order of Canada), Wayne Gretzky (Companion), and Céline Dion. The motto of the Order of Canada is 'Desiderantes meliorem patriam' — Latin for 'They desire a better country.'",
    ai_explanation: "The Order of Canada is like Canada's 'thank you medal' for extraordinary citizens. If you've done something truly remarkable for Canada — in science, arts, sports, community service — the government can recognize you with this honour. There are three levels, with Companion being the highest, like the A+ of Canadian recognition.",
