    title: "Chinese Canadian History", slug: "chinese-canadian-history", access_level: "pro",
    short_definition: "The history of Chinese immigration to Canada, marked by significant contributions (CPR construction) and systemic discrimination (Chinese Head Tax and Exclusion Act).",
    full_description: "Chinese immigrants began arriving in Canada in large numbers during the 1858 Gold Rush and were crucial to building the Canadian Pacific Railway (1881–1885). Despite this contribution, the federal government imposed a Chinese Head Tax in 1885 — a fee of $50 (later raised to $500) charged only to Chinese immigrants. Between 1885 and 1923, the government collected approximately $23 million in head taxes. In 1923, the Chinese Immigration Act (called the Exclusion Act) virtually banned all Chinese immigration.\n\nThe Exclusion Act was repealed in 1947. In 2006, Prime Minister Stephen Harper issued a formal apology to Chinese Canadians for the head tax. The Chinese Canadian community has made enormous contributions to Canadian culture, business, cuisine, and public life. Today, Chinese Canadians are one of the largest ethnic communities in Canada, concentrated in Vancouver and Toronto.",
    ai_explanation: "Chinese Canadians helped build Canada's railway, which united the country from coast to coast — and then were punished for it with discriminatory taxes and bans. This dark chapter is an important reminder of Canada's imperfections and why equality rights in the Charter matter so much today.",
