    title: "Canadian Pacific Railway", slug: "canadian-pacific-railway", access_level: "pro",
    short_definition: "Canada's first transcontinental railway, completed in 1885, which physically united Canada from the Atlantic to the Pacific and was central to Confederation.",
    full_description: "The Canadian Pacific Railway (CPR) was a condition of British Columbia joining Confederation in 1871 — BC demanded a rail link to the east. The CPR was built between 1881 and 1885 under the leadership of William Van Horne and was financed through a combination of government subsidies and private investment. The railway was completed when Donald Smith drove the famous 'last spike' at Craigellachie, British Columbia, on November 7, 1885.\n\nThe CPR's construction relied heavily on approximately 15,000 Chinese workers who did the most dangerous work blasting through the Rocky Mountains and Selkirk Mountains. The CPR opened up western Canada to settlement, enabled the transport of prairie wheat to markets, and made it possible for the federal government to suppress the Northwest Rebellion of 1885. It remains one of the most significant nation-building projects in Canadian history.",
    ai_explanation: "The CPR was basically the glue that held early Canada together. Without a railway connecting east and west, BC would have had no reason to join Confederation. Imagine Canada ending at Manitoba — the CPR made Canada truly 'coast to coast.' It was dangerous, expensive, and only possible with the labour of thousands of Chinese workers who never received proper recognition.",
