    title: "Japanese Canadian Internment", slug: "japanese-canadian-internment", access_level: "pro",
    short_definition: "The forced relocation and internment of approximately 22,000 Japanese Canadians during World War II, considered one of Canada's most shameful acts.",
    full_description: "Following Japan's attack on Pearl Harbor (December 7, 1941), the Canadian government — fearing espionage — ordered the forced removal of all people of Japanese origin living within 100 miles of the BC coast. Approximately 22,000 Japanese Canadians (including Canadian-born citizens) were relocated to interior BC, Alberta, Manitoba, and Ontario. Their homes, businesses, fishing boats, and property were seized and sold. Many were sent to work camps or internment sites in isolated locations.\n\nThe internment continued until 1949, four years after the war ended. In 1988, Prime Minister Brian Mulroney issued a formal apology to Japanese Canadians and the government provided $21,000 in compensation to surviving internees. The internment is studied today as an example of wartime racism and a reminder of why the Charter of Rights and Freedoms — and its protections against discrimination — are so important.",
    ai_explanation: "During WWII, the Canadian government was so afraid of Japanese Canadians being spies that they took away 22,000 people's homes, businesses, and freedom — even though most were Canadian citizens. It was wrong then, and the government apologized decades later. This is why Canada's Charter says the government CANNOT discriminate based on national or ethnic origin.",
    related_terms: r([{title:"Charter of Rights and Freedoms",slug:"charter-of-rights-and-freedoms"},{title:"Equality Rights",slug:"equality-rights"},{title:"World War II",slug:"world-war-ii"}]),
