    title: "Underground Railroad", slug: "underground-railroad", access_level: "pro",
    short_definition: "A network of secret routes and safe houses that helped enslaved people from the United States escape to freedom in Canada in the 1800s.",
    full_description: "The Underground Railroad was not a literal railroad but a secret network of routes, safe houses, and people who helped enslaved African Americans escape from slave states in the US to free states and Canada. Canada, where slavery had been abolished in 1833 under the British Empire, was a destination for freedom-seekers. It is estimated that between 30,000 and 40,000 enslaved people escaped to Canada via the Underground Railroad, primarily between the 1850s and the American Civil War.\n\nKey figures included Harriet Tubman, who made 19 trips and guided 300 people to freedom, spending significant time in St. Catharines, Ontario. Freedom-seekers settled in communities across Ontario, Nova Scotia, and elsewhere in Canada. The community of Buxton (near Chatham, Ontario) and other Black communities became thriving settlements. After the American Civil War ended slavery in the US (1865), many returned south, but thousands remained and their descendants are part of Canada's African-Canadian communities today.",
    ai_explanation: "The Underground Railroad was like a secret network of brave people helping enslaved Americans escape to freedom in Canada. Because Britain abolished slavery before the US did, Canada was a destination for freedom. Thousands of people risked everything to escape to Canada, and many communities of their descendants still exist today.",
