    title: "Black Loyalists", slug: "black-loyalists", access_level: "pro",
    short_definition: "Black Americans who supported Britain during the American Revolution and came to Canada (mainly Nova Scotia and New Brunswick) after 1783 seeking freedom.",
    full_description: "Black Loyalists were Black Americans — many of whom had been enslaved — who sided with the British during the American Revolution (1775–1783), often in exchange for promises of freedom. When Britain lost the war, approximately 3,500 Black Loyalists came to Nova Scotia and New Brunswick in 1783 as part of the broader Loyalist migration. They were among the first Black settlers in Atlantic Canada.\n\nDespite their service, Black Loyalists faced severe discrimination. They received smaller land grants than white Loyalists, and many were refused grants altogether. In 1792, about 1,200 Black Loyalists chose to leave for Sierra Leone in West Africa, frustrated by discrimination. Those who remained, along with later arrivals, formed the foundation of African-Nova Scotian communities that persist today. Their history is an important part of Canada's diverse cultural heritage.",
    ai_explanation: "Black Loyalists were Black people who came to Canada after the American Revolution because Britain promised them freedom. Canada was supposed to be a fresh start — but they faced discrimination even here. Their story is important because it shows that Canada's diversity goes back more than 200 years, and that the fight for true equality has been long.",
