    title: "Durham Report", slug: "durham-report", access_level: "pro",
    short_definition: "An 1839 report by Lord Durham recommending responsible government and the union of Upper and Lower Canada after the Rebellions of 1837–38.",
    full_description: "After the Rebellions of 1837–38 in Upper Canada (Ontario) and Lower Canada (Quebec), Britain sent Lord Durham to investigate. His 1839 report, often called 'the report that launched a thousand ships,' made two key recommendations: (1) unite Upper and Lower Canada under a single legislature to assimilate French Canadians into English culture; and (2) grant 'responsible government' — meaning the elected assembly rather than an appointed governor would control domestic affairs.\n\nThe Act of Union of 1840 implemented the union, creating the Province of Canada. Responsible government was achieved in 1848–49. Durham's report significantly underestimated French Canadian resilience — rather than assimilating, French Canadian culture and language thrived and became an enduring pillar of Canadian identity. The achievement of responsible government was a critical step toward Confederation in 1867.",
    ai_explanation: "Lord Durham's report was like a troubleshooter's assessment after Canada's failed rebellions of 1837. He recommended letting Canadians govern themselves (responsible government) and uniting French and English Canada. His plan to assimilate French Canadians failed spectacularly — French Canada didn't disappear. But responsible government succeeded and paved the way to Confederation.",
    related_terms: r([{title:"Confederation",slug:"confederation"},{title:"Responsible Government",slug:"responsible-government"},{title:"Official Languages",slug:"official-languages"}]),
    quiz_question: "What was Lord Durham's most significant recommendation in his 1839 report?",
