    full_description: "On May 20, 1980, Quebec held a referendum on sovereignty-association — a proposal by the Parti Québécois government of Premier René Lévesque for Quebec to become politically sovereign (independent) while maintaining economic association with Canada. The question asked voters to give the government a mandate to negotiate such an arrangement. Prime Minister Pierre Trudeau campaigned vigorously for the 'No' side.\n\nThe 'No' side won with 59.56% of the vote. Trudeau had promised Quebecers that a 'No' vote would lead to constitutional renewal, which contributed to the patriation of the Constitution in 1982 — though Quebec never signed the Constitution Act, 1982. The referendum revealed the deep currents of Quebec nationalism that would resurface in a second referendum in 1995.",
