    short_definition: "A near-miss 1995 vote in which Quebec voters rejected independence from Canada by only 50.58% to 49.42% — the closest vote in Canadian democratic history.",
    full_description: "On October 30, 1995, Quebec held its second referendum on independence. The 'Yes' side, led by PQ Premier Jacques Parizeau and Bloc Québécois leader Lucien Bouchard, proposed that Quebec become an independent country. The campaign was extremely close and emotionally charged. The 'No' side, led by Quebec Liberal leader Daniel Johnson and supported by Prime Minister Jean Chrétien, narrowly won with 50.58% of the vote — a margin of only about 50,000 votes out of nearly 5 million cast.\n\nThe result shocked Canadians. The federal government responded with the Clarity Act (2000), which established that any future Quebec independence referendum must ask a clear question and achieve a clear majority. The 1995 referendum remains the closest Canada has come to breaking apart. It fundamentally changed how Ottawa approached Quebec's place in Confederation and reinforced discussions about recognition of Quebec's distinct society.",
