    full_description: "In May 1914, the Japanese steamship Komagata Maru arrived in Vancouver harbour carrying 376 passengers from British India — most were Sikhs, and all were British subjects. However, Canada had passed regulations specifically designed to prevent South Asian immigration, including the 'continuous journey' rule requiring immigrants to travel without stopping, which was impossible from India.\n\nFor two months, passengers were not allowed to disembark. Most were refused entry and the ship was forced to return to India. On arrival in Calcutta, British colonial authorities fired on the passengers, killing about 20. The Canadian government formally apologized in 2016 when Prime Minister Justin Trudeau addressed the House of Commons and expressed the government's deep regret for turning away these British subjects based on race. The Komagata Maru incident is a defining example of Canada's historical discriminatory immigration policies.",
