    short_definition: "Canada's central bank, responsible for monetary policy, issuing currency, and promoting the economic and financial welfare of Canada.",
    full_description: "The Bank of Canada was established in 1934 and began operations in 1935. It is a Crown corporation that serves as Canada's central bank. Its primary mandate is to promote the economic and financial welfare of Canada. It does this through monetary policy (setting the key interest rate to control inflation), issuing Canadian bank notes (paper currency), serving as the government's fiscal agent, and promoting financial system stability.\n\nThe Bank of Canada's inflation target is to keep the Consumer Price Index (CPI) at 2% — the midpoint of a 1% to 3% range. The Governor of the Bank of Canada is appointed by the federal Cabinet. Unlike the US Federal Reserve, the Bank of Canada operates with a clear mandate and inflation target, which it has followed since 1991. Decisions made by the Bank of Canada affect mortgage rates, business borrowing, and the Canadian dollar's value.",
    ai_explanation: "The Bank of Canada is Canada's financial control centre. It doesn't serve individual customers like a regular bank — instead, it controls how much money flows through the economy by setting interest rates. Raise rates and borrowing gets more expensive, slowing inflation. Lower rates and borrowing gets cheaper, stimulating growth. Every time the Bank moves its rate, Canadians feel it in their mortgages.",
