    title: "GST and HST", slug: "gst-hst", access_level: "pro",
    short_definition: "The Goods and Services Tax (GST) is a federal consumption tax of 5%; in some provinces it is combined with provincial sales tax into the Harmonized Sales Tax (HST).",
    full_description: "The Goods and Services Tax (GST) is a 5% federal tax on most goods and services sold in Canada, introduced in 1991 under Prime Minister Brian Mulroney. It replaced the hidden Manufacturers' Sales Tax. In five provinces — Ontario, Nova Scotia, New Brunswick, Newfoundland and Labrador, and Prince Edward Island — the GST is combined with provincial sales tax into the Harmonized Sales Tax (HST), ranging from 13% to 15%.\n\nMost goods and services are taxable, but certain items are exempt (zero-rated), including basic groceries, prescription drugs, and medical devices. Low-income Canadians can claim a GST/HST credit — a quarterly payment from the CRA to help offset the tax. The GST is one of Canada's most significant revenue sources, generating tens of billions of dollars annually for federal programs.",
    ai_explanation: "GST is the tax you see at the bottom of receipts — the government's cut on most things you buy. In most provinces, it's combined with provincial sales tax into the HST. Basic groceries aren't taxed (because food is essential), but most things you buy at a store will have GST/HST added. If your income is low enough, the government sends you a quarterly rebate to help cover it.",
