    title: "Equalization Payments", slug: "equalization-payments", access_level: "pro",
    short_definition: "Federal transfer payments made to less prosperous provinces to ensure they can provide comparable public services at comparable tax rates.",
    full_description: "Equalization is a federal program — enshrined in Section 36 of the Constitution Act, 1982 — that transfers money to provinces whose fiscal capacity (ability to raise revenue) falls below a national average. The goal is to ensure that all Canadians, regardless of which province they live in, can access roughly comparable levels of public services (such as health care and education) without having their provincial government impose undue tax burdens.\n\nThe formula for calculating equalization is complex, based on each province's ability to generate revenue from five standard tax bases. Provinces like Ontario, Alberta, and British Columbia typically do not receive equalization. Provinces like Quebec, Manitoba, and the Atlantic provinces typically do receive equalization payments. The program does not come from any specific province's taxes — it comes from the federal government's general revenue, meaning all Canadians contribute through their federal taxes.",
    ai_explanation: "Equalization is Canada's way of making sure where you live doesn't determine what services you receive. If you live in a rich province (like Alberta), your province can fund great hospitals and schools easily. If you live in a poorer province, equalization payments from the federal government help top up the budget so Canadians everywhere get comparable services. It's one of the ways Canada balances being a federation with being fair.",
    related_terms: r([{title:"Federal Government",slug:"federal-government"},{title:"Provincial Government",slug:"provincial-government"},{title:"Medicare",slug:"medicare"}]),
