    title: "Legal Rights (Charter)", slug: "legal-rights-charter", access_level: "pro",
    short_definition: "Sections 7–14 of the Charter of Rights and Freedoms, guaranteeing rights in criminal proceedings such as the right to life, liberty, and security, and the right to a fair trial.",
    full_description: "Sections 7 to 14 of the Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedoms collectively comprise 'Legal Rights,' which protect individuals in dealings with the justice system. Section 7 guarantees the right to life, liberty, and security of the person. Section 8 protects against unreasonable search and seizure. Section 9 protects against arbitrary detention. Section 10 guarantees the right to be told why you are arrested and the right to retain a lawyer (including the right to be informed of legal aid). Section 11 covers rights of persons charged with an offence, including the right to be tried within a reasonable time and to be presumed innocent. Sections 12–14 protect against cruel treatment, right to an interpreter, and protection against self-incrimination.\n\nThese rights apply to all people in Canada — not just citizens — and are most often invoked in criminal proceedings. Evidence obtained in violation of Charter rights may be excluded from trial under Section 24(2). Legal Rights are a cornerstone of Canada's justice system.",
    ai_explanation: "Legal Rights in the Charter are your protection when you deal with the police and courts. Think of them as rules the government must follow when it accuses you of something: they must tell you why you're arrested, you can call a lawyer right away, they can't search you without good reason, and you're innocent until proven guilty. These rights exist for everyone in Canada, not just citizens.",
