    title: "Meech Lake Accord", slug: "meech-lake-accord", access_level: "pro",
    short_definition: "A 1987 package of constitutional amendments intended to bring Quebec into the Constitution Act, 1982, which failed after Manitoba and Newfoundland did not ratify it by the 1990 deadline.",
    full_description: "The Meech Lake Accord was negotiated in 1987 by Prime Minister Brian Mulroney and all ten provincial premiers to address Quebec's conditions for signing the Constitution Act, 1982 (which Quebec had refused to sign). The key provisions included recognizing Quebec as a 'distinct society' within Canada, giving provinces more power over immigration, limiting the federal spending power in areas of provincial jurisdiction, and giving provinces the right to opt out of constitutional amendments.\n\nThe Accord required ratification by all ten provincial legislatures within three years. However, it failed in June 1990 when the Manitoba legislature was unable to pass it (Elijah Harper, a Cree MLA, withheld unanimous consent required for fast-tracking) and Newfoundland Premier Clyde Wells called off a ratification vote. The failure of the Meech Lake Accord boosted Quebec nationalism and Bloc Québécois support, contributing to the 1995 Quebec referendum.",
    ai_explanation: "Meech Lake was Canada's attempt to finally get Quebec to sign the Constitution. The deal would have recognized Quebec as a 'distinct society.' But it needed ALL provinces to say yes, and it failed when a Manitoba Indigenous MLA, Elijah Harper, refused to let it pass quickly — partly to protest the lack of Indigenous consultation. Its failure made Quebec's independence movement stronger.",
