    short_definition: "A 1992 package of constitutional reforms that was defeated in a national referendum, addressing Quebec, Indigenous self-government, and Senate reform.",
    full_description: "After the failure of the Meech Lake Accord, the federal and provincial governments tried again with a broader set of constitutional proposals called the Charlottetown Accord (1992). It addressed multiple issues: recognizing Quebec's distinct society, guaranteed 25% of Commons seats for Quebec, an elected 'Triple-E' Senate (equal, elected, effective), and recognition of an inherent right to Indigenous self-government.\n\nUnlike Meech Lake, the Charlottetown Accord was put to a national referendum on October 26, 1992. It was defeated, with about 54% voting 'No.' The 'No' vote came from a coalition of those who thought it gave Quebec too much, those who thought it gave too little, and those who opposed Senate reform. The defeat of the Charlottetown Accord effectively ended the era of mega-constitutional negotiations in Canada for many years.",
