    short_definition: "The 36 political leaders who attended the Confederation conferences (Charlottetown 1864, Quebec 1864, London 1866) and brought about the creation of Canada in 1867.",
    full_description: "The Fathers of Confederation were the delegates from the British North American colonies who negotiated the terms of Confederation at three conferences: Charlottetown, PEI (September 1864), Quebec City (October 1864), and London, England (1866–67). There were 36 delegates in total. Key figures included John A. Macdonald (Canada West/Ontario), George-Étienne Cartier (Canada East/Quebec), Samuel Leonard Tilley (New Brunswick), Charles Tupper (Nova Scotia), and Leonard Tilley.\n\nThe conferences resulted in the 72 Quebec Resolutions, which became the framework for the British North America Act (1867). The three original provinces were Canada (divided into Ontario and Quebec), Nova Scotia, and New Brunswick. Macdonald became the first Prime Minister. Their portraits appear on Canadian currency and their names are commemorated across the country.",
    ai_explanation: "Think of the Fathers of Confederation as the founding team of Canada — the politicians who sat down in 1864 and designed how Canada would work. There were 36 of them, and they had to convince their colonies that joining together made more sense than staying separate. They created the blueprint (the Quebec Resolutions) that became Canada's founding law in 1867.",
    related_terms: r([{title:"Confederation",slug:"confederation"},{title:"John A. Macdonald",slug:"john-a-macdonald"},{title:"British North America Act",slug:"british-north-america-act"}]),
