    full_description: "Pierre Elliott Trudeau was one of the most consequential Prime Ministers in Canadian history. Known for 'Trudeaumania' when he first took office in 1968, he served two terms (1968–1979 and 1980–1984). His major achievements include implementing the Official Languages Act (1969), making Canada officially bilingual; the War Measures Act during the October Crisis (1970); introducing multiculturalism as federal policy (1971); and most significantly, patriating Canada's Constitution in 1982 and entrenching the Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedoms.\n\nTrudeau's constitutional vision met significant resistance from Quebec (which has never signed the 1982 Constitution) and from Indigenous leaders who felt their rights weren't adequately protected. His government's imposition of the National Energy Program (NEP) in 1980 created lasting resentment in Alberta. Despite controversies, Trudeau is consistently ranked among Canada's greatest Prime Ministers for transforming Canada's national identity through bilingualism, multiculturalism, and the Charter.",
    ai_explanation: "Pierre Trudeau fundamentally changed what it means to be Canadian. Before him, Canada didn't have a clear national identity distinct from Britain. He gave Canada the Charter of Rights (protecting everyone's freedoms), made French and English truly equal as official languages, and proclaimed multiculturalism as Canada's identity. His father-son legacy continues with Justin Trudeau.",
