    full_description: "Nellie McClung (1873–1951) was one of Canada's most influential social reformers. Born in Ontario and raised in Manitoba, she became a teacher and then a celebrated novelist. She was a passionate advocate for women's suffrage, temperance, and social reform. In 1916, she helped win women the right to vote in Manitoba — the first province to grant women provincial voting rights. She later served as a Member of the Alberta Legislative Assembly (1921–1926).\n\nMcClung was one of the Famous Five who petitioned the Supreme Court in the Persons Case, winning the landmark 1929 ruling that women are legally persons. She represented Canada at the League of Nations in Geneva in 1938. Her famous quote, 'Never retract, never explain, never apologize — get things done and let them howl,' captures her determined spirit. McClung is one of only six women on Canada's Walk of Fame in Ottawa.",
