    short_definition: "A Canadian athlete and cancer activist who, after losing his leg to cancer, ran his 'Marathon of Hope' across Canada in 1980 to raise money for cancer research.",
    full_description: "Terry Fox (1958–1981) was a 21-year-old from Port Coquitlam, British Columbia, when he began his Marathon of Hope on April 12, 1980. Having lost his right leg to osteosarcoma (bone cancer) at 18, Fox ran across Canada on a prosthetic leg, averaging 42 km (26 miles) a day. His goal was to run the full width of Canada — roughly 8,000 km — to raise $1 for every Canadian for cancer research.\n\nFox ran for 143 days, covering 5,373 km (3,339 miles) through Newfoundland, Nova Scotia, New Brunswick, Quebec, Ontario, and into Thunder Bay, Ontario, when he was forced to stop on September 1, 1980, after cancer spread to his lungs. He died on June 28, 1981. The Terry Fox Foundation continues his legacy, having raised over $850 million for cancer research. Annual Terry Fox Runs are held in September in communities across Canada and around the world.",
    ai_explanation: "Terry Fox is one of Canada's greatest heroes — not for anything political, but for sheer courage and selflessness. He had already lost his leg to cancer, but instead of giving up, he decided to run across an entire continent to help other cancer patients. He didn't finish, but his legacy has raised over $850 million for cancer research. He shows what individual determination can achieve.",
