    full_description: "John George Diefenbaker (1895–1979) was a Saskatchewan lawyer and Conservative politician who led Canada's government from 1957 to 1963. In the 1958 election, his Progressive Conservative party won the largest majority government in Canadian history up to that time (208 of 265 seats). Diefenbaker was a passionate defender of civil liberties. His most lasting legislative achievement was the Canadian Bill of Rights (1960), a federal statute protecting individual rights and freedoms — a precursor to the Charter of Rights and Freedoms (1982).\n\nDiefenbaker appointed Ellen Fairclough as Secretary of State in 1957, making her Canada's first female federal cabinet minister. He also extended the franchise (right to vote) to Indigenous Canadians in 1960. His government is also known for the Avro Arrow controversy — the cancellation of the CF-105, a supersonic jet fighter program, in 1959.",
