    full_description: "Alberta became a province on September 1, 1905, along with Saskatchewan. It is located between BC and Saskatchewan, bordered by Montana to the south. Alberta is Canada's fourth-most populous province and is known for its oil sands — one of the world's largest oil reserves, centred around Fort McMurray. Oil and natural gas production have made Alberta historically one of Canada's wealthiest provinces, which does not receive equalization payments.\n\nThe province is also known for agriculture (beef cattle, wheat), the Rocky Mountains along its western border (Banff and Jasper National Parks), and a conservative political culture. Calgary hosts the famous Calgary Stampede rodeo every July. Edmonton, the capital, is a gateway to Canada's north. Alberta does not have a provincial sales tax. Indigenous peoples including Blackfoot, Cree, and Métis peoples have deep historical roots in the region.",
