    short_definition: "A disastrous Allied raid on the French coastal town of Dieppe on August 19, 1942, in which approximately 3,600 of 5,000 Canadian soldiers were killed, wounded, or captured.",
    full_description: "Operation Jubilee — the Dieppe Raid — was an Allied amphibious assault on the German-occupied port of Dieppe, France, on August 19, 1942. Of the approximately 6,000 assault troops, nearly 5,000 were Canadians from the 2nd Canadian Infantry Division. The raid was a military disaster. Within hours, about 3,623 Canadian soldiers were killed, wounded, or captured. Only a small fraction reached and briefly held their objectives before being repulsed.\n\nDespite the catastrophic losses, the lessons learned at Dieppe — about the need for better intelligence, naval fire support, and coordination — influenced the planning for the D-Day invasion of Normandy in June 1944. The Dieppe Raid is remembered as a tragic example of Canadian courage and sacrifice. The failure at Dieppe also demonstrated that Canadian forces were capable of learning and adapting, which they proved at D-Day and in the Liberation of the Netherlands.",
    ai_explanation: "Dieppe was a tragic military disaster — 3,600 Canadian soldiers were killed, captured, or wounded in a single day in 1942. It was poorly planned and the Germans were ready for them. But the harsh lessons learned helped make D-Day successful two years later. Canadians remember Dieppe as a sacrifice that, while devastating, was not entirely in vain.",
