    title: "D-Day and Liberation of Normandy", slug: "d-day-normandy", access_level: "pro",
    short_definition: "On June 6, 1944, Canadian forces stormed Juno Beach as part of the D-Day invasion of Normandy, France — one of Canada's most celebrated military achievements.",
    full_description: "Operation Overlord — the D-Day invasion on June 6, 1944 — was the largest seaborne invasion in history. Canadian forces were assigned to storm Juno Beach, one of five landing sectors. Approximately 14,000 Canadian soldiers landed on Juno Beach that day, suffering about 1,074 casualties (359 killed). Canadian forces advanced further inland on D-Day than any other Allied force, capturing Courseulles-sur-Mer and other objectives.\n\nFollowing D-Day, Canadian forces played a crucial role in the liberation of northwestern Europe. Canada liberated the Scheldt Estuary (1944), which opened the port of Antwerp and allowed Allied supply lines to function. Most significantly, Canada liberated much of the Netherlands from German occupation in 1944–45. The Dutch people have maintained a deep gratitude to Canada ever since — every year the Netherlands sends 20,000 tulips to Ottawa as a symbol of thanks.",
