    full_description: "The Parliament Buildings sit on Parliament Hill, a limestone escarpment overlooking the Ottawa River. The main building is the Centre Block, which contains the House of Commons chamber, the Senate chamber, and the Peace Tower — a 92-metre clock tower with the Carillon bells and the Memorial Chamber honouring fallen Canadians. The original Centre Block was destroyed by fire in 1916 (only the Library of Parliament survived) and was rebuilt and completed in 1922.\n\nThe Centre Block underwent a major renovation starting in 2018. During this time, Parliament used the West Block and Senate of Canada Building. East Block houses offices and committee rooms and is preserved as a heritage site. The Library of Parliament, built in 1876 in a circular Gothic-revival style, survived the 1916 fire and is one of Ottawa's most beautiful buildings. Parliament Hill also hosts ceremonies, citizenship events, and Canada Day celebrations.",
