    short_definition: "An annual plan published by IRCC setting Canada's targets for permanent resident admissions across economic, family, and refugee categories.",
    full_description: "Canada's Immigration Levels Plan is a multi-year targets document that IRCC tables in Parliament each November. It sets out how many permanent residents Canada plans to admit in the coming years across three main categories: Economic (skilled workers, entrepreneurs, caregivers — approximately 60%), Family Reunification (spouses, parents, grandparents — approximately 25%), and Refugees and Humanitarian (government-assisted refugees, privately sponsored refugees — approximately 15%).\n\nCanada's immigration targets have grown significantly. The 2023 targets were set at 465,000 new permanent residents, with plans for similar levels through 2025. Canada's immigration policy is driven by demographic needs: aging population, low birth rate, labour force shortages, and the need to sustain pension and health care systems. The Levels Plan is a key policy document because it determines not just numbers but also which programs (Express Entry, PNP, etc.) will have the highest number of spots.",
    ai_explanation: "Each year, Canada publishes a plan saying exactly how many immigrants it wants to admit and in what categories. It's Canada saying: 'we need THIS many skilled workers, THIS many family members, THIS many refugees.' Unlike most countries, Canada has a transparent, positive attitude toward immigration — it's not just tolerated, it's planned for and seen as essential.",
