    short_definition: "An Express Entry stream for people with at least one year of skilled Canadian work experience who want to become permanent residents.",
    full_description: "The Canadian Experience Class (CEC) is one of the three federal programs managed under Express Entry. It is designed for people who are already in Canada on a temporary basis (work permit holders, international graduates) and have accumulated at least one year of skilled work experience in Canada within the last three years. The key advantage is that applicants are already adapted to Canadian society, and their Canadian work experience is highly valued.\n\nCEC applicants must meet language requirements (CLB 5 for NOC TEER 0 and 1 jobs, CLB 4 for NOC TEER 2 and 3 jobs). There is no minimum points score requirement independent of Express Entry — all CEC-eligible candidates enter the Express Entry pool and compete in draws. CEC draws can be program-specific (CEC-only draws) or all-program draws. CEC applicants typically have higher CRS scores because Canadian work experience earns significant points in the Comprehensive Ranking System.",
    ai_explanation: "Canadian Experience Class is basically Canada saying: 'If you've already been working here for a year and are integrated into our society — you're probably a good fit for permanent residence.' It rewards people who are already contributing to Canada. International students who graduate and work here are prime candidates — Canada wants to keep the talented people it has already trained.",
